WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.000
O-N-A-L-A, LA Times Meetup, if you're tweeting this,

00:05.720 --> 00:08.200
O-N-A-L-A-T hashtag.

00:09.860 --> 00:14.240
Welcome, we're, you know, this is kind of new ground for us.

00:14.240 --> 00:19.240
We're, the LA Times is happy to host this event,

00:19.960 --> 00:23.220
to get together more digital journalists in a room

00:23.220 --> 00:26.760
than in this town, than we've seen in a while.

00:26.760 --> 00:31.760
And I just welcome you and haven't prepared any remarks,

00:34.240 --> 00:36.600
so I'm gonna turn it over to Julie,

00:36.600 --> 00:39.200
who perhaps has prepared.

00:40.640 --> 00:42.840
Yeah, there's this idea it's gonna get better from here,

00:42.840 --> 00:44.340
but you might be disappointed.

00:45.560 --> 00:46.980
Thank you guys so much for coming.

00:46.980 --> 00:48.720
I really, I have no idea who you all are,

00:48.720 --> 00:49.920
and that's so exciting.

00:51.320 --> 00:54.880
I just, we wanted to start doing more of these

00:54.920 --> 00:57.120
types of things to get people together,

00:57.120 --> 01:01.560
with wine and snacks, and some information.

01:01.560 --> 01:03.120
So we hope to keep doing these things,

01:03.120 --> 01:05.520
and actually Robert is gonna talk about that.

01:07.200 --> 01:09.360
Hello, I'm Robert Hernandez.

01:09.360 --> 01:10.960
My Twitter handle is webjournalist,

01:10.960 --> 01:12.520
some people know me from that.

01:12.520 --> 01:13.920
Stand over here by the screen.

01:15.560 --> 01:20.560
We, Kim and I started O-N-A-L-A about two years ago or so.

01:21.640 --> 01:24.400
I'm a professor at USC, I moved back to LA

01:24.520 --> 01:27.760
about three years ago, and I wanted to meet other web nerds,

01:28.720 --> 01:33.640
and my virtual office wife and I met.

01:33.640 --> 01:35.920
And since then, we've been trying to get people together

01:35.920 --> 01:39.600
to kind of connect and nerd out and support each other.

01:39.600 --> 01:43.760
And normally it tends to be with like 48 hours notice

01:43.760 --> 01:46.280
at the Redwood, we haven't seen each other in a while,

01:46.280 --> 01:47.920
so we meet and drink.

01:47.920 --> 01:51.360
And thanks to Julie, we have this formal presentation

01:51.360 --> 01:54.560
or programming that we hope to do every couple of months.

01:54.560 --> 01:56.080
We wanna do it more, you know,

01:56.080 --> 01:58.400
the formal sit down learning,

01:58.400 --> 02:00.240
mind exchange every couple of months,

02:00.240 --> 02:03.360
and then some social meetup every other month

02:03.360 --> 02:04.520
or something like that.

02:05.760 --> 02:07.600
It's great to see so many folks,

02:07.600 --> 02:09.320
I know a chunk of you are from LA Times,

02:09.320 --> 02:11.400
and some new folks I don't know as well.

02:12.720 --> 02:16.040
Hope that this becomes a more routine thing,

02:16.040 --> 02:18.240
because it's better, journalism is better,

02:18.240 --> 02:19.600
the community is better served

02:19.600 --> 02:21.200
when we kind of share our knowledge,

02:21.680 --> 02:24.240
and experience, and be nerds together.

02:25.440 --> 02:26.960
Thank you for coming.

02:26.960 --> 02:30.360
Hi, I'm Kim, I am Robert's virtual office spouse.

02:31.480 --> 02:34.720
And my only job is to tell you that if your newsroom,

02:34.720 --> 02:38.560
or if you have ideas on what we should do for this programming,

02:38.560 --> 02:41.120
we are willingly accepting them.

02:41.120 --> 02:44.040
Just find one of us before, after the programming,

02:44.040 --> 02:47.040
pitch your idea, or if you have a cool bar we should go to.

02:47.040 --> 02:49.120
And also become a member of ONA,

02:49.120 --> 02:50.880
because it's kind of awesome.

02:50.880 --> 02:51.880
Who is a member of ONA?

02:51.880 --> 02:53.600
I'm on the board for the national.

02:54.880 --> 02:59.560
So, ONA, if you don't know, tends to be DC focused,

02:59.560 --> 03:02.600
or historically it's been East Coast focused.

03:02.600 --> 03:05.600
I got elected onto the board almost two years ago,

03:05.600 --> 03:09.800
I'm up for reelection if I decide to run again this year.

03:09.800 --> 03:11.640
But one of the things that I wanted to do

03:11.640 --> 03:13.560
when I was on the board was to make sure

03:13.560 --> 03:17.080
West Coast was represented in some form.

03:17.080 --> 03:19.460
But we need members to participate.

03:20.380 --> 03:22.380
Outside of a great conference,

03:22.380 --> 03:24.340
there's other things like this

03:24.340 --> 03:25.740
to kind of justify your membership,

03:25.740 --> 03:30.180
but at the very least, as an ONA person,

03:30.180 --> 03:33.500
it's this group that is growing.

03:33.500 --> 03:37.260
I think it's the largest international web journalism,

03:37.260 --> 03:39.340
online journalism organization.

03:40.420 --> 03:42.340
And it's just growing and getting better,

03:42.340 --> 03:43.940
but only made better through its members.

03:43.940 --> 03:45.140
So please consider joining.

03:45.140 --> 03:48.700
I think it's like 75 bucks or something, go on.

03:48.780 --> 03:49.620
Please.

03:54.020 --> 03:55.580
Martin's, sorry guys.

03:55.580 --> 03:59.380
Martin's gonna introduce the wonderful data desk people,

03:59.380 --> 04:00.700
but if everyone could give Martin a hand

04:00.700 --> 04:03.380
because he did a great job getting all of this together.

04:03.380 --> 04:08.060
Thank you.

04:10.540 --> 04:15.460
So, without any further ado whatsoever,

04:15.460 --> 04:17.500
Megan Garvey is our,

04:19.380 --> 04:22.660
she's somehow attached to the data desk.

04:22.660 --> 04:25.860
No one's really sure, but she'll tell you more about it.

04:25.860 --> 04:27.700
She provides the alcohol.

04:27.700 --> 04:30.180
I do do that, which is probably

04:30.180 --> 04:31.700
why anyone listens to me at all.

04:31.700 --> 04:33.300
They pointed that out to me.

04:33.300 --> 04:36.340
So I'm Megan Garvey, and I've been at the LA Times

04:36.340 --> 04:37.340
since 1998.

04:37.340 --> 04:40.020
I started out as a very traditional print journalist,

04:40.020 --> 04:43.280
and I can't even remember the first time I saw our website.

04:43.280 --> 04:46.040
And then a few years ago,

04:46.040 --> 04:51.040
I proposed an idea to do a database of California's war dead.

04:52.360 --> 04:53.880
So the men and women who'd been killed

04:53.880 --> 04:55.200
in the wars in California.

04:55.200 --> 04:57.480
And at the time I'd worked with Doug Smith,

04:57.480 --> 04:59.880
who's sitting, Doug, raise your hand.

04:59.880 --> 05:00.720
There he is.

05:01.560 --> 05:04.600
I had worked with Doug Smith on some data oriented projects

05:04.600 --> 05:06.560
in the past, but at the time there was really no way

05:06.560 --> 05:08.600
to publish most of what we did online.

05:08.600 --> 05:11.520
So a lot of work went into stuff that ended up

05:11.520 --> 05:13.960
as print graphics or informed stories,

05:13.960 --> 05:16.580
but was never really made accessible to the public.

05:16.580 --> 05:19.400
So I went to Doug and I said, I have this idea.

05:19.400 --> 05:22.000
And he said, two months ago, I would have said,

05:22.000 --> 05:24.240
great idea, no way, we can't do it.

05:24.240 --> 05:27.360
But we just hired someone named Ben Welsh,

05:27.360 --> 05:28.680
and I think you should meet him.

05:28.680 --> 05:32.160
So that was the beginning of, I don't know what it is, but.

05:32.160 --> 05:33.320
A beautiful friendship.

05:33.320 --> 05:36.320
Yes, a beautiful friendship.

05:36.320 --> 05:39.480
So the data desk here is really not,

05:39.480 --> 05:40.800
it's funny because a lot of people think of it

05:40.920 --> 05:42.480
as an official thing, but really what it is

05:42.480 --> 05:45.360
is a group of people from different sections of the paper

05:45.360 --> 05:46.860
who've kind of banded together.

05:46.860 --> 05:51.040
I don't know if it's on a life boat or a ship or raft

05:51.040 --> 05:52.520
or I don't know, a parachute.

05:52.520 --> 05:54.320
I'm not sure exactly what it is, but.

05:55.520 --> 05:59.120
Yes, only Doug, some kind of motorcycle.

06:00.120 --> 06:02.960
But the nice thing about that is that we work together

06:02.960 --> 06:05.840
by choice, and so it's been a lot of fun.

06:05.840 --> 06:08.400
And we have a weekly meeting we call show and tell

06:08.400 --> 06:11.080
where we often argue and fight with each other,

06:11.080 --> 06:13.560
but at the end of the day, come up with good ideas.

06:13.560 --> 06:16.240
And Ben is gonna come up and talk more

06:16.240 --> 06:17.960
about what it is that we actually do

06:17.960 --> 06:19.640
and what the data desk is.

06:19.640 --> 06:21.480
And then we're gonna take a look at a project

06:21.480 --> 06:23.840
that actually was born out of a lot of fighting

06:23.840 --> 06:26.280
and arguing and discussing.

06:26.280 --> 06:27.360
So Ben, come on up.

06:27.360 --> 06:32.360
Hello, okay, so does this work when I stand this far away?

06:40.560 --> 06:42.120
Can everybody hear me?

06:42.120 --> 06:43.160
Okay, good.

06:43.160 --> 06:45.900
My name's Ben Welsh, I work at the LA Times,

06:45.900 --> 06:48.120
and I do this stuff like Megan said.

06:48.120 --> 06:50.280
And our little band of people has been together

06:50.280 --> 06:52.680
for a few years and we've put together some stuff,

06:52.680 --> 06:54.960
and I'm gonna run you through kind of what we do

06:54.960 --> 06:57.000
and our work, add some examples of that,

06:57.000 --> 07:00.160
and then hand it off to Anthony to get more specific, okay?

07:00.160 --> 07:02.880
So I'm gonna go through a lot of stuff really fast,

07:02.880 --> 07:04.880
and if there's anything you have questions about

07:04.880 --> 07:06.000
or you wanna know more about,

07:06.000 --> 07:09.240
just raise your hand or say, hey, Ben, or whatever,

07:09.240 --> 07:12.160
because this is informal enough that we can stop

07:12.160 --> 07:14.400
and have a conversation, at least I hope so, okay?

07:14.400 --> 07:16.480
So feel free when the time comes.

07:16.480 --> 07:19.040
So the name of my talk is What is a Data Desk?

07:19.040 --> 07:21.400
And I'm gonna walk you through kind of what we do.

07:21.400 --> 07:23.080
If you wanna check out the slides later,

07:23.080 --> 07:25.480
they're at this URL here at the bottom,

07:25.520 --> 07:30.000
which is lat.ms slash Who is Data Desk?

07:31.080 --> 07:32.400
Okay, so like Megan said,

07:32.400 --> 07:35.280
the Data Desk is an informal team of reporters

07:35.280 --> 07:39.000
and programmers here in downtown LA at the LA Times,

07:39.000 --> 07:40.800
emphasis on team.

07:40.800 --> 07:44.560
And what we do basically in like a really oversimplified way

07:44.560 --> 07:47.160
is we turn databases into news,

07:47.160 --> 07:49.160
though in some cases, news is into databases,

07:49.160 --> 07:50.480
but that's like a whole other thing.

07:50.480 --> 07:53.160
But usually we turn databases into news.

07:53.160 --> 07:56.080
So like an example would be the California War Dead database

07:56.080 --> 07:59.160
that Megan talked about where there's a record

07:59.160 --> 08:01.640
for every casualty and then all those go,

08:01.640 --> 08:03.640
every one of those casualties gets its own page

08:03.640 --> 08:05.720
on the internet and you sort of turn the database

08:05.720 --> 08:07.760
into something on a news website

08:07.760 --> 08:10.200
that is different from a story, right?

08:10.200 --> 08:12.840
Other examples we've done that are more notorious

08:12.840 --> 08:17.080
include the rating of how well every teacher at LAUSD

08:17.960 --> 08:20.040
improves the test scores of their students,

08:20.920 --> 08:23.560
every person who's on the Hollywood Walk of Fame

08:23.560 --> 08:24.600
would be another example too.

08:24.600 --> 08:25.920
This is where you build the database

08:25.920 --> 08:29.680
that is sort of news in its way, right?

08:29.680 --> 08:32.280
That means that we write code, right?

08:32.280 --> 08:34.280
Like real code, we don't just talk about it,

08:34.280 --> 08:37.120
we do it and we do it all day, right?

08:37.120 --> 08:40.440
And I like to write a lot of Django, that's my favorite,

08:40.440 --> 08:42.680
and we do a lot of our projects in Django,

08:42.680 --> 08:44.640
which is a Python framework

08:44.640 --> 08:47.080
that makes it easier to build websites.

08:47.080 --> 08:49.280
So it's the Python programming language,

08:49.360 --> 08:50.640
it's all these handy shortcuts

08:50.640 --> 08:53.880
that make doing stuff a lot easier, right?

08:53.880 --> 08:55.960
And this is an example of a stack

08:55.960 --> 08:58.720
that you would use to put out a Python website

08:58.720 --> 08:59.800
that actually a lot of our sites

08:59.800 --> 09:01.640
kind of work a little bit like this.

09:01.640 --> 09:04.000
But that's not all that we use, right?

09:04.000 --> 09:06.000
There's a lot of other technologies that we use,

09:06.000 --> 09:07.200
there's people different from me

09:07.200 --> 09:09.240
who have different opinions and use different software

09:09.240 --> 09:12.520
like Doug, who uses a lot of SAS,

09:12.520 --> 09:14.760
though I'm trying to win him over, gradually.

09:14.760 --> 09:17.400
And there's other projects that you work on

09:17.560 --> 09:21.000
where certain tools are better than other tools, right?

09:21.000 --> 09:22.360
And so these are just some examples

09:22.360 --> 09:24.680
of different technologies we use.

09:24.680 --> 09:27.560
I'm sort of, I'm showing my age

09:27.560 --> 09:30.560
with the DHTML reference, right?

09:30.560 --> 09:33.720
Is anyone here old enough to remember that, right?

09:33.720 --> 09:37.120
Yes, right, I like to remind the younger developers,

09:37.120 --> 09:40.560
the internet had buzzwords before you were born, right?

09:40.560 --> 09:45.560
And anyway, but we also still can write in English,

09:45.560 --> 09:48.360
we didn't lose that when we became computer programmers.

09:48.360 --> 09:51.240
I know some people think that or that like they're somehow,

09:51.240 --> 09:53.320
they're like two, the two things don't go together,

09:53.320 --> 09:54.840
but that's not true at all.

09:54.840 --> 09:56.480
We remain the people we were before,

09:56.480 --> 09:57.840
we learned to program, right?

09:57.840 --> 09:59.920
And these are some examples of some front page stories

09:59.920 --> 10:01.840
that have come out of our work, right?

10:01.840 --> 10:03.080
And so now here we go through it.

10:03.080 --> 10:05.720
One thing we do, we make a lot of maps, right?

10:05.720 --> 10:07.400
That's the globe lobby you've just seen.

10:07.400 --> 10:11.320
I call it Art Deco Data Vis, right?

10:11.320 --> 10:13.100
In a certain sense, but we have a site

10:13.100 --> 10:15.080
where we map out the most recent crimes

10:15.080 --> 10:18.040
in the city of Los Angeles by neighborhood.

10:18.040 --> 10:21.080
Ken, it built a beautiful site to show every homicide

10:21.080 --> 10:24.600
in LA County over the last what, six years?

10:24.600 --> 10:25.440
Something like that.

10:25.440 --> 10:27.520
Something like that and Malloy and Sarah Artilani

10:27.520 --> 10:30.560
do a ton of awesome work to keep that up to date.

10:30.560 --> 10:32.080
You know, there's, we do a lot of other data

10:32.080 --> 10:34.640
by neighborhood, this is the census,

10:34.640 --> 10:37.640
this is every marijuana dispensary in Los Angeles, right?

10:37.640 --> 10:39.960
And we're putting out a lot of maps like this.

10:39.960 --> 10:42.020
Yeah, I know, this is not minimal, right?

10:42.020 --> 10:44.280
This is whatever the opposite of minimal is.

10:44.280 --> 10:48.360
And this is a very recent map I'm geeked about

10:48.360 --> 10:51.080
because this is one that Ken did to coincide

10:51.080 --> 10:54.120
with the LA riots with a lot of research from Malloy

10:54.120 --> 10:56.640
that shows everyone who died during the LA riots.

10:56.640 --> 10:58.380
And one that's really interesting in itself,

10:58.380 --> 10:59.680
but one little geeky thing

10:59.680 --> 11:01.680
that maybe some users wouldn't notice

11:01.680 --> 11:03.420
is this is the first LA Times map

11:03.420 --> 11:05.840
that has custom map tiles.

11:05.840 --> 11:07.840
It doesn't rely on Google maps for its service.

11:07.840 --> 11:09.400
Ken has done a lot of awesome work

11:09.400 --> 11:11.920
using OpenStreetMap and Tile Mill

11:11.920 --> 11:15.660
to get us on the path to escape from Google maps, right?

11:15.660 --> 11:17.580
And so this was our Sputnik.

11:19.700 --> 11:22.880
So besides maps, we also do investigations, right?

11:22.880 --> 11:25.980
You know, a lot of data work that people think of

11:25.980 --> 11:29.900
as data journalism or as this brand new web thing

11:29.900 --> 11:32.580
that never existed before because whatever,

11:32.580 --> 11:36.160
because big data is a buzzword now, it isn't true.

11:36.160 --> 11:38.860
I mean, computer assisted reporting is a geeky term,

11:38.860 --> 11:40.820
but it's been around for 25 years

11:40.820 --> 11:43.060
and people like Doug and Sandy and Malloy

11:43.060 --> 11:45.180
who are in this room have used technical skills

11:45.180 --> 11:47.800
to do investigations for literally decades

11:47.800 --> 11:49.180
and we still do that, right?

11:49.180 --> 11:51.180
So a recent example is Malloy and Ken

11:51.180 --> 11:53.080
worked on a story about all those people

11:53.080 --> 11:54.920
who then died in the riots

11:54.920 --> 11:56.960
and the cases that are still open, right?

11:56.960 --> 11:57.920
That's a recent one.

11:59.360 --> 12:02.020
Last year we did a big investigation about autism

12:02.020 --> 12:03.760
in California and across the country

12:03.760 --> 12:05.640
that included a lot of data analysis

12:05.640 --> 12:08.480
done by Doug and Sandy Poindexter

12:08.480 --> 12:10.500
who I don't think is here.

12:10.500 --> 12:12.320
But that's really good stuff if you haven't seen it.

12:12.320 --> 12:15.640
That's at latimes.com slash autism.

12:15.640 --> 12:17.460
I'd recommend the whole package.

12:17.460 --> 12:19.500
And then, you know, when Occupy hit last year,

12:19.500 --> 12:22.240
I was able to use some data to do a quickie census

12:22.240 --> 12:24.280
of the people who were arrested, right?

12:24.280 --> 12:27.200
So we still do a lot of these classic things

12:27.200 --> 12:30.060
that have to do with data analysis and investigations.

12:30.060 --> 12:32.200
Right, we also train robot reporters.

12:32.200 --> 12:33.780
That's another thing that we do, right?

12:33.780 --> 12:34.860
So what does that mean, right?

12:34.860 --> 12:36.660
Well, so for instance, this is a blog post

12:36.660 --> 12:38.440
from the Homicide Report, right?

12:38.440 --> 12:39.920
Which is a site that Ken built

12:39.920 --> 12:42.000
and is staffed by some people here at the Times

12:42.000 --> 12:43.720
where we write a blog post for every person

12:43.720 --> 12:45.520
who is killed in Los Angeles County, right?

12:45.520 --> 12:47.280
We're the only ones who do this.

12:47.280 --> 12:50.340
Now, you know, that's a lot of work and we can't staff it.

12:50.340 --> 12:52.000
But one way to make it less work

12:52.000 --> 12:53.740
is to automate some of the easy stuff.

12:53.740 --> 12:56.200
So as soon as the spreadsheet comes in from the corner

12:56.200 --> 12:59.800
every Monday, every Monday, Malloy, right,

12:59.800 --> 13:01.360
feeds it into the system.

13:01.360 --> 13:04.100
And the system, just based on the basic data fields

13:04.100 --> 13:06.800
that are in the spreadsheet, the age of the person,

13:06.800 --> 13:10.440
their race, their name, the location of where they died.

13:10.440 --> 13:12.920
Is there anything else that's, how they died

13:12.920 --> 13:14.960
that like you ever stabbed or shot?

13:14.960 --> 13:17.280
Ken has written some code that will take those rows

13:17.280 --> 13:18.360
and fields from the spreadsheet

13:18.360 --> 13:20.800
and write the first paragraph to every story.

13:20.800 --> 13:22.860
So before any human has to do any work,

13:22.860 --> 13:26.320
well, barring Malloy, you know, right?

13:26.320 --> 13:29.640
Right, sorry, Malloy's going by pseudonym tonight,

13:29.640 --> 13:30.480
I'm sorry.

13:32.200 --> 13:35.440
That initial paragraph is automatically written

13:35.440 --> 13:36.440
with no human intervention.

13:36.440 --> 13:39.120
And that's not all we do, but it's the bare minimum, right?

13:39.120 --> 13:41.480
So we have something up as soon as possible.

13:41.480 --> 13:44.760
And then if or when we're able to assign more resources

13:44.760 --> 13:47.840
to cover homicide, that person can then write through that

13:47.840 --> 13:49.800
and add more material onto the post, right?

13:49.800 --> 13:51.320
Which in this case was Robert Lopez,

13:51.320 --> 13:53.680
who was in the room but had to leave, right?

13:53.680 --> 13:56.280
And we take that approach to a lot of different things.

13:56.280 --> 13:58.840
Here's a blog post that appears a couple times a week,

13:58.840 --> 14:02.920
every time new crime data is fed to us by the LAPD,

14:02.920 --> 14:05.200
where we have an algorithm that goes through it,

14:05.240 --> 14:08.240
identifies the neighborhoods that are having unusually

14:08.240 --> 14:10.440
high amount of crime, and writes this post

14:10.440 --> 14:11.920
and makes this map and does all this

14:11.920 --> 14:14.020
with no human work whatsoever, right?

14:15.280 --> 14:17.280
We have pages for all these neighborhoods about crime

14:17.280 --> 14:20.200
and all this text is automatically written by algorithm.

14:20.200 --> 14:22.640
And one, the example I think is the coolest

14:22.640 --> 14:26.400
is one that Ken did, where he has a computer application

14:27.500 --> 14:30.320
that sits on top of the USGS's database

14:30.320 --> 14:31.680
and notification system.

14:31.680 --> 14:34.560
And every time an earthquake notification goes out,

14:34.560 --> 14:37.640
it processes that, has certain filters it puts it through

14:37.640 --> 14:39.560
before it decides it's newsworthy, right?

14:39.560 --> 14:42.760
And that's his news judgment written into code, right?

14:42.760 --> 14:45.040
And then if it's a newsworthy earthquake

14:45.040 --> 14:47.040
according to the algorithm, this blog post,

14:47.040 --> 14:50.040
this map, all this automatically written, fed into the blog,

14:50.040 --> 14:53.280
email sent to the editor, get on it, right?

14:53.280 --> 14:55.300
I bet people in this room have covered earthquakes.

14:55.300 --> 14:57.640
Somebody has probably, who doesn't work here?

14:57.640 --> 15:00.520
And what, yeah, and what usually happens, right?

15:00.520 --> 15:02.760
What usually happens, either it hits the wire

15:02.760 --> 15:04.480
and you're like, oh shit, we missed it.

15:05.480 --> 15:07.440
Or somebody says, did you feel that?

15:08.400 --> 15:11.320
And then everybody is clicking around on the USGS website.

15:11.320 --> 15:12.360
Where is it, where's that link?

15:12.360 --> 15:13.280
They have 18 versions.

15:13.280 --> 15:15.520
Which one is this one, it fought, fought, fought, fought, fought.

15:15.520 --> 15:17.600
Right?

15:17.600 --> 15:19.880
And in this case, in our case, that's Ron Lynn, right?

15:19.880 --> 15:22.160
That's the, for the LA Times people.

15:22.160 --> 15:24.620
But we don't need to do that anymore.

15:24.620 --> 15:25.800
The computer can do it.

15:25.800 --> 15:28.000
And Ron Lynn could work on his investigation

15:28.000 --> 15:30.080
into corruption at the Coliseum Commission

15:30.080 --> 15:32.880
and freakin' make our city a better place to live.

15:32.880 --> 15:33.720
You know what I mean?

15:33.960 --> 15:36.200
You worry about this stupid earthquake, right?

15:36.200 --> 15:37.520
Unless an earthquake hits the city.

15:37.520 --> 15:39.320
Unless an earthquake hits the city.

15:39.320 --> 15:43.880
One that surpasses a certain barrier on your algorithm, right?

15:43.880 --> 15:45.960
So anyway, that's a cool example of that.

15:45.960 --> 15:48.960
We also build open source tools, right?

15:48.960 --> 15:50.580
We have a GitHub account.

15:50.580 --> 15:52.060
Who here is on GitHub?

15:53.680 --> 15:55.920
Right, so GitHub is like the social network

15:55.920 --> 15:57.320
for computer programmers.

15:57.320 --> 16:00.480
It's where you put your open source code up on there

16:00.480 --> 16:02.400
and your friends can follow it, right?

16:02.400 --> 16:04.400
And when you make an update, you patch some code,

16:04.400 --> 16:06.720
they get a little Facebook update, right?

16:06.720 --> 16:09.600
And you can, it's true.

16:09.600 --> 16:12.800
It sounds, and it's awesome.

16:12.800 --> 16:14.380
So I don't know what's so funny.

16:14.380 --> 16:16.760
But like if you wanna like learn to code

16:16.760 --> 16:18.840
or get into programming or have something better

16:18.840 --> 16:22.680
than a crappy PDF resume, I'd recommend

16:22.680 --> 16:25.000
that you put your code up on GitHub

16:25.000 --> 16:26.720
and share it with the world and you know,

16:26.720 --> 16:29.320
start participating in what is really a historic

16:29.320 --> 16:31.080
like thing in computer programming.

16:31.080 --> 16:32.920
And so we have a GitHub account and you know,

16:32.920 --> 16:34.960
we're not anything special, but we've got you know,

16:34.960 --> 16:37.680
10 or 12 open source projects that are out there

16:37.680 --> 16:40.280
that we're you know, trying to build a community around.

16:40.280 --> 16:41.760
And this is just a couple examples.

16:41.760 --> 16:44.640
One is we have a framework for quickly making

16:44.640 --> 16:45.840
an interactive table.

16:45.840 --> 16:47.520
You know, you wanna make, you wanna search and sort

16:47.520 --> 16:50.520
and filter really simply a table on deadline.

16:50.520 --> 16:52.680
We've built a framework internally that can do that

16:52.680 --> 16:53.640
and open sourced it.

16:53.640 --> 16:55.200
It's called Table Stacker.

16:55.200 --> 16:57.580
It's being used by about a half dozen different newsrooms.

16:57.580 --> 16:59.260
This here on MinPost.

17:00.260 --> 17:03.580
Here we had, Ken made a really great library,

17:03.580 --> 17:06.980
a wrapper on top of the AP's election data service,

17:06.980 --> 17:09.660
that like FTP that they dump everything on,

17:09.660 --> 17:11.520
that makes it really, really easy for you

17:11.520 --> 17:13.580
to write Python code to put the data out

17:13.580 --> 17:14.860
and put it up on your website.

17:14.860 --> 17:17.020
And after he did that, probably what,

17:17.020 --> 17:18.980
six or seven different news organizations

17:18.980 --> 17:21.660
have just stopped writing that same redundant code

17:21.660 --> 17:23.660
themselves and instead are using ours.

17:23.660 --> 17:26.580
And not only are they getting something out of it,

17:26.580 --> 17:29.320
they're contributing patches, fixes and features

17:29.320 --> 17:32.060
to our code so we don't have to do any work

17:32.060 --> 17:34.780
and they're making our life easier, right?

17:34.780 --> 17:36.540
Okay, so that's the benefit.

17:36.540 --> 17:38.420
Right, another example is this is a fun one.

17:38.420 --> 17:41.020
It's like auto complete for address searches.

17:41.020 --> 17:44.140
It uses the Google Geocoder as you type in an address.

17:44.140 --> 17:45.740
That's the LA Times address.

17:45.740 --> 17:47.580
It starts to suggest different places, right,

17:47.580 --> 17:48.420
which is kind of fun.

17:48.420 --> 17:49.860
But there's a lot of other ones if you go to

17:49.860 --> 17:53.540
github.com slash data desk.

17:53.540 --> 17:56.420
Okay, and Anthony who's gonna follow me up here,

17:56.620 --> 17:59.660
this is him on Twitter, Anthony J. Pesci, so follow him.

17:59.660 --> 18:00.920
He does a lot of different work here,

18:00.920 --> 18:03.220
but one thing that he focuses on

18:03.220 --> 18:05.180
and has really had a lot of success with

18:05.180 --> 18:09.700
is taking some of our print or less interactive

18:09.700 --> 18:12.380
graphic stuff and making it more interactive

18:12.380 --> 18:15.220
or more up to speed with the latest stuff on the web.

18:15.220 --> 18:17.280
And he's gonna show you in depth one of those examples,

18:17.280 --> 18:19.780
but here's some other stuff that he's done.

18:19.780 --> 18:23.020
We have a template now to make a California counties map

18:23.020 --> 18:24.580
just using a spreadsheet.

18:24.580 --> 18:25.740
We just upload the spreadsheet,

18:25.940 --> 18:27.900
boom, we've got the map like in 10 or 15 minutes,

18:27.900 --> 18:29.500
the power of automation, right?

18:29.500 --> 18:31.140
These are maps we have to do a lot.

18:31.140 --> 18:33.420
Same thing for the US states, he's made that.

18:34.420 --> 18:37.540
This is a recent one that he did for a big Sunday package

18:37.540 --> 18:40.100
that's more custom, that's about the boulevards

18:40.100 --> 18:43.620
of Los Angeles, our architecture critic is walking people

18:43.620 --> 18:45.100
through the major changes on them.

18:45.100 --> 18:47.940
And this sits on top of this big story he's written,

18:47.940 --> 18:49.060
which you can find where?

18:49.060 --> 18:49.900
What's the URL?

18:51.180 --> 18:53.340
LAtimes.com slash boulevards.

18:53.340 --> 18:54.780
This just came out on Sunday.

18:56.380 --> 18:59.380
We have templates now for doing these big long graphics

18:59.380 --> 19:02.140
where you sort of have a little nav that changes

19:02.140 --> 19:04.260
as you scroll down and you can link to.

19:04.260 --> 19:06.940
And the template is done in a way so that the graphics staff

19:06.940 --> 19:09.660
who couldn't build a page like this without some training,

19:09.660 --> 19:12.740
all they have to do is upload the images at a certain size

19:12.740 --> 19:14.540
and boom, they've got a graphic like this.

19:14.540 --> 19:15.660
So since they've done this,

19:15.660 --> 19:17.380
since we introduced this template,

19:17.380 --> 19:19.980
they've probably done what a dozen of these?

19:19.980 --> 19:23.060
Right, and it takes graphics work that in many cases

19:23.060 --> 19:25.380
either would have been in print,

19:25.380 --> 19:28.100
appreciated by print readers and then hated on the web

19:28.100 --> 19:29.460
because it was in a crappy template

19:29.460 --> 19:31.020
or just totally lost on the web

19:31.020 --> 19:33.460
and puts it in a really just simple template

19:33.460 --> 19:35.780
that makes it like actually halfway enjoyable

19:35.780 --> 19:38.380
for the internet, more than halfway Anthony, like, you know,

19:38.380 --> 19:40.060
like, but yeah.

19:40.060 --> 19:43.660
And it's really been amazing to me to see the response

19:43.660 --> 19:45.660
on social media where it's just graphics

19:45.660 --> 19:47.260
that were in a sense routine

19:47.260 --> 19:49.700
for our really talented graphics artists

19:49.700 --> 19:51.700
that were just ignored online,

19:51.700 --> 19:54.220
suddenly can have a lot of success on social media

19:54.220 --> 19:56.260
just because there's something to link to

19:56.260 --> 19:58.660
that people don't hate, right?

19:58.660 --> 20:00.140
And so like this particular graphic,

