Robert.

22:17.420 --> 22:20.020
Do you take two seconds not to buy a Kickstarter project?

22:20.020 --> 22:22.620
Okay, so outside of my work in the last few weeks

22:22.620 --> 22:25.780
I launched a website which is called Past Pages.

22:25.780 --> 22:28.460
It is at pastpages.org.

22:28.460 --> 22:30.680
And it is essentially, let's see if we can fire it up here

22:30.680 --> 22:34.460
maybe, let's run with scissors as Sarah Cohen would call it.

22:34.460 --> 22:35.420
We're gonna use the live web.

22:35.420 --> 22:37.340
And essentially every hour it goes through

22:37.340 --> 22:40.300
several dozen internet news site homepages

22:40.300 --> 22:41.940
and takes snapshots of them

22:41.940 --> 22:44.420
where it will hopefully archive them forever.

22:44.440 --> 22:45.820
A lot of you are probably familiar

22:45.820 --> 22:47.980
with the Wayback Machine at archive.org

22:47.980 --> 22:49.620
which is a really awesome site.

22:49.620 --> 22:51.820
However, it doesn't update often enough

22:51.820 --> 22:54.180
or regular enough to really do thorough study

22:54.180 --> 22:56.060
of what media is up to.

22:56.060 --> 22:59.140
And so the goal of this site is to really regularly capture

22:59.140 --> 23:01.660
as many media homepages as possible

23:01.660 --> 23:04.940
so that academics like Robert can have a great resource

23:04.940 --> 23:08.760
to study what happens on these pages over time.

23:08.760 --> 23:10.780
Like what if you could do an academic study

23:10.780 --> 23:12.860
of every photo of Michelle Obama

23:12.860 --> 23:15.860
that was used by the media before this election, right?

23:15.860 --> 23:16.700
Or whatever, right?

23:16.700 --> 23:18.420
You can start thinking about different ways you can do it.

23:18.420 --> 23:21.120
Or when we have a financial collapse next year,

23:21.120 --> 23:23.660
how the Wall Street Journal decides to cover that, right?

23:23.660 --> 23:25.580
So it has an academic use.

23:25.580 --> 23:28.020
Second, hopefully I think it has a media criticism use.

23:28.020 --> 23:29.740
For instance, last week the Drudge Report

23:29.740 --> 23:33.180
posted a false story about how President Obama

23:33.180 --> 23:36.300
had fabricated things in his memoir.

23:36.300 --> 23:38.720
It was only up for several minutes and then it disappeared

23:38.720 --> 23:40.580
but the screenshots on this site captured it.

23:40.580 --> 23:42.640
So if someone wanted to write a story pointing out

23:42.720 --> 23:44.500
that Matt Drudge likes to hype things

23:44.500 --> 23:46.440
that usually aren't true,

23:46.440 --> 23:48.840
they could use that as evidence.

23:48.840 --> 23:50.120
Right, so, and also, you know,

23:50.120 --> 23:51.420
if you work at one of these news outlets

23:51.420 --> 23:53.600
and you just wanna keep track of what's going on

23:53.600 --> 23:54.720
with your homepage over time,

23:54.720 --> 23:56.560
I hope it can be useful to you.

