24:15.640 --> 24:16.760
or what I've screwed up with it,

24:16.760 --> 24:18.120
please feel free to tell me afterwards.

24:18.120 --> 24:20.440
It is pastpages.org.

24:20.440 --> 24:21.480
Any other questions?

24:26.040 --> 24:28.880
No, okay, well, with that, I'll hand it over to Anthony.

24:32.040 --> 24:35.840
This is one of the more recent projects that I've worked on

24:35.840 --> 24:40.200
and it is a sort of interactive web graphic

24:40.200 --> 24:44.960
for looking at how super PACs are spending their money

24:45.800 --> 24:48.080
in the primary race.

24:49.560 --> 24:52.040
Does everybody know what a super PAC is?

24:52.040 --> 24:55.040
So a political action committee is sort of

24:55.040 --> 24:57.320
supposedly independent organization

24:57.320 --> 25:01.480
that can spend money during a campaign, right?

25:01.480 --> 25:04.360
Super PACs, a result of a recent Supreme Court decision,

25:04.360 --> 25:06.160
can spend unlimited amounts of money

25:07.160 --> 25:09.320
called independent expenditures

25:10.240 --> 25:15.240
and can receive unlimited donations from interested parties.

25:17.560 --> 25:22.560
In many cases, super PACs are not supposed to be

25:24.200 --> 25:27.160
tied to a candidate or controlled by a candidate in any way.

25:27.160 --> 25:28.200
They're supposed to be independent,

25:28.200 --> 25:31.960
but many of these PACs are run by people

25:31.960 --> 25:35.960
who are friends and advisors and work with

25:36.040 --> 25:36.880
some of the candidates.

25:36.880 --> 25:41.880
So we've tried to describe some of the super PAC activity

25:45.320 --> 25:49.480
happening in the selection using this graphic.

25:49.480 --> 25:50.920
Before I get into it too much,

25:50.920 --> 25:55.160
I wanted to show ProPublica has done some excellent work

25:55.160 --> 26:00.160
looking at super PACs also, both spending and contributors

26:06.960 --> 26:10.360
and the New York Times has done some work

26:10.360 --> 26:11.560
on super PACs as well,

26:11.560 --> 26:16.560
looking at some of the top contributors among other things.

26:18.680 --> 26:21.120
We took a look at some of this work

26:21.120 --> 26:25.360
and decided that we wanted to contribute something

26:25.360 --> 26:29.800
to the story about what was happening with super PACs

26:29.800 --> 26:31.720
in this election season.

26:31.720 --> 26:34.680
And we sort of sat in a meeting on a Friday afternoon

26:34.960 --> 26:38.200
and argued for about an hour.

26:39.400 --> 26:44.400
And sort of decided that, after looking at some of the data

26:44.920 --> 26:48.800
and decided that what's probably going to be

26:48.800 --> 26:51.760
the most interesting story or at least a very interesting

26:51.760 --> 26:56.760
story is how are these candidates using affiliated

26:58.600 --> 27:03.600
but not affiliated PACs to spend money on other candidates?

27:04.560 --> 27:08.720
And we sort of discovered, tell you right here,

27:08.720 --> 27:11.320
56% of all of these expenditures

27:11.320 --> 27:13.640
have been spent opposing candidates.

27:13.640 --> 27:16.600
And you can see from looking at this graphic

27:16.600 --> 27:21.180
that Mitt Romney spent an exorbitant amount of money,

27:21.180 --> 27:24.000
almost $21 million opposing Santorum

27:24.000 --> 27:27.320
and almost $19 million opposing Newt Gingrich

27:29.520 --> 27:32.040
and in large part knocked them out of the race.

27:33.600 --> 27:36.400
So you don't really get that looking here.

27:36.400 --> 27:38.800
You don't really get that looking here.

27:38.800 --> 27:41.320
And I think one of the key things we decided

27:42.240 --> 27:45.360
both specifically about this graphic and in general

27:45.360 --> 27:49.000
about the kinds of presentations we wanna do

27:49.000 --> 27:52.420
for a database is it needs to tell a story.

27:53.320 --> 27:56.440
So for us it was really important to have something

27:56.440 --> 28:00.240
that was quick and relatively digestible

28:00.240 --> 28:04.160
where you could come here and see what was happening

28:04.160 --> 28:06.520
in a way that you couldn't if you just received

28:06.520 --> 28:11.520
sort of a dump of a database onto a webpage.

28:11.840 --> 28:13.080
And you can dive in.

28:13.080 --> 28:16.220
We have a timeline where you can look at spending over time.

28:19.560 --> 28:24.200
So you can see Romney's really concerned about Newt Gingrich

28:26.080 --> 28:28.060
in January and February.

28:28.060 --> 28:33.060
Newt didn't do so well.

28:35.940 --> 28:38.880
He starts contributing against Santorum.

28:38.880 --> 28:41.060
Most of these large jumps you see by the way

28:41.060 --> 28:43.260
are right before key state primaries.

28:44.880 --> 28:47.300
And then, okay there goes Santorum

28:47.300 --> 28:49.960
and lately now he's spending money on himself.

28:52.220 --> 28:54.820
You can see very recently Obama has started spending

28:54.820 --> 28:58.080
some money against Romney.

29:01.060 --> 29:03.020
We still have people like Herman Cain and Huntsman

29:03.020 --> 29:04.200
and Rick Perry here.

29:06.060 --> 29:08.100
But you can dive in.

29:08.100 --> 29:10.340
So it tells a story initially when you come to the page

29:10.340 --> 29:13.440
to look at it and it allows you to sort of come in

29:13.440 --> 29:18.440
and explore and look at how the spending has evolved

29:18.940 --> 29:20.780
over the course of the primary season.

29:22.580 --> 29:23.780
Any questions so far?

29:25.140 --> 29:26.420
Okay.

29:26.420 --> 29:30.520
We also have a spreadsheet available of the biggest donors.

29:32.420 --> 29:36.380
Very, very wealthy and politically interested people

29:36.380 --> 29:39.760
who are contributing to one or more super PACs

29:39.760 --> 29:43.900
and in incredibly large amounts of money.

29:43.900 --> 29:46.780
This would not have been really possible before

29:46.780 --> 29:48.020
or at least not like this.

29:50.320 --> 29:53.100
So I'm gonna tell you a little bit about how we get

29:53.100 --> 29:56.380
and clean and process the data and store it in a database

29:56.380 --> 30:00.020
and then I'm gonna talk very briefly about some of the code

30:00.020 --> 30:00.860
that went into it.

30:03.380 --> 30:06.340
This is, we get all of our data from the FEC,

30:06.340 --> 30:07.900
the Federal Election Commission.

30:10.460 --> 30:14.340
If you come to this page, FEC.gov slash data slash

30:14.340 --> 30:16.220
independent expenditure dot do,

30:17.860 --> 30:21.500
you can look at the latest filings

30:21.500 --> 30:25.580
and this is updated what, every day or two more?

30:25.580 --> 30:29.180
It depends on the committee but it's on a 24, 48 hour cycle.

30:29.180 --> 30:31.540
They update each file, each committee file.

30:33.460 --> 30:36.900
So the FEC has kindly provided this interface

30:36.900 --> 30:39.900
where you can come and you can start to explore the data

30:40.960 --> 30:44.620
and you can download it here with a CSV as a CSV

30:44.620 --> 30:48.840
which is what we do which gets you this.

30:52.460 --> 30:55.500
Not a particularly handy format

30:55.500 --> 30:58.940
although you can sort of see what they have, right?

30:58.940 --> 31:01.900
So there's a candidate name and some sort of ID

31:01.900 --> 31:06.900
and another ID, a state maybe, which office it is,

31:07.940 --> 31:09.820
how much money, the date.

31:11.100 --> 31:12.380
There's a lot of data here.

31:12.380 --> 31:15.280
It's somewhat indecipherable what's going on.

31:16.280 --> 31:21.280
So we wrote a program to come to this page,

31:21.640 --> 31:25.520
download the CSV, store it into a database

31:25.520 --> 31:30.520
and from there, there's a little bit of a manual process

31:30.520 --> 31:34.120
for cleaning up the data that Malloy can talk more about.

31:35.200 --> 31:39.240
But essentially the problem is when you come to this page

31:39.240 --> 31:40.600
and you download the spreadsheet,

31:40.600 --> 31:44.040
if you were to do a sum on this expended amount

31:44.040 --> 31:46.980
on the spreadsheet, it wouldn't actually be accurate

31:46.980 --> 31:51.980
because you can amend an FEC filing.

31:52.860 --> 31:56.920
So if you file one report that says you spent 20 bucks

31:56.920 --> 31:58.920
on advertising, you can come back and you can say,

31:58.920 --> 32:00.980
oops, I forgot a line item.

32:00.980 --> 32:04.480
I actually spent 20 bucks here and five bucks there

32:04.480 --> 32:07.720
and that just gets inserted into the top of the spreadsheet

32:07.720 --> 32:10.760
with all of the new expenses and all of the old expenses

32:11.600 --> 32:15.560
and you kind of have to manually go through

32:15.560 --> 32:18.720
and figure out what's what and what amended what

32:18.720 --> 32:23.720
and that's this column, AMN underscore IND, right?

32:25.800 --> 32:28.120
And if it's an N, it means it has not been amended

32:28.120 --> 32:31.480
and if it's an A1 or an A2, it means it has been.

32:31.480 --> 32:33.080
So you kind of have to figure out

32:36.400 --> 32:38.720
where these expenses line up.

32:38.720 --> 32:41.040
And Molloy, do you wanna come up for a sec?

32:41.040 --> 32:43.600
So Molloy is our fabulous researcher

32:43.600 --> 32:47.280
who spends her days toiling away doing this.

32:47.280 --> 32:49.200
So go to the FEC.

32:49.200 --> 32:52.080
So the one thing you'll notice on this site,

32:52.080 --> 32:54.440
this is the FEC site where you can look at,

32:54.440 --> 32:57.200
this is an example of associated buildings

32:57.200 --> 33:01.560
and it's every day they file a report

33:01.560 --> 33:03.400
and then they file quarterly or monthly

33:03.400 --> 33:04.960
depending on what cycle they're on

33:04.960 --> 33:09.960
but you can see here that that filing amended

33:12.680 --> 33:13.680
the one that filed it.

33:13.680 --> 33:15.560
So back in Anthony's database,

33:15.560 --> 33:18.360
what I have to do is I have to say,

33:18.360 --> 33:23.280
I wanna keep the 77820 and I wanna get rid

33:23.280 --> 33:25.000
of everything else because if I don't,

33:25.000 --> 33:26.560
then I'm gonna do this double count

33:26.560 --> 33:29.200
and there's not a nice way to do it

33:29.200 --> 33:34.080
except manually look at the file, the spreadsheet

33:34.080 --> 33:37.480
and look at those 5D numbers and then come back in here

33:37.480 --> 33:39.880
and get rid of the ones I don't want

33:39.880 --> 33:41.680
and keep the ones I do want.

33:41.680 --> 33:44.400
And it doesn't take a lot of time

33:44.400 --> 33:47.480
but I have noticed before that I've not been careful.

33:47.480 --> 33:49.480
I thought, oh, I've gotten rid of everything

33:49.480 --> 33:50.920
and then I'm like looking at things

33:50.920 --> 33:53.320
and I've forgotten to eliminate stuff.

33:53.320 --> 33:55.720
It goes by pretty quickly.

33:55.720 --> 33:57.680
You can do it in a pretty quick span of time

33:57.680 --> 34:00.360
and get rid of the things you don't need to have.

34:00.360 --> 34:04.800
So this is sort of a peek at our admin for this site.

34:04.800 --> 34:08.440
It's powered by a database of all of these expenditures.

34:10.400 --> 34:13.280
The one thing back on this file that we're starting with,

34:13.280 --> 34:16.720
this is Super PACs working to,

34:18.520 --> 34:21.440
for the presidential campaign and congressional campaign too.

34:21.440 --> 34:23.360
So that's our next thing we're gonna work on.

34:23.360 --> 34:25.520
We're gonna look at how Super PACs

34:25.520 --> 34:29.480
are supporting congressional races in California.

34:29.480 --> 34:31.520
But basically the workflow on something like this

34:31.520 --> 34:34.680
we've set up is I have this script

34:34.680 --> 34:38.080
that can go and fetch the spreadsheet from the FEC,

34:38.080 --> 34:41.140
look at everything, store it in our database,

34:42.000 --> 34:44.800
but we can't publish that live right away.

34:44.800 --> 34:48.360
You need to actually take a minute, look at the data,

34:48.360 --> 34:50.640
fix any of the amended filings,

34:50.640 --> 34:53.400
make sure it passes the smell test

34:53.400 --> 34:55.680
and then hit a publish button.

34:55.680 --> 34:58.880
So we have an interface where in this admin,

34:58.920 --> 35:01.520
you click a button and it pulls the data from the FEC.

35:01.520 --> 35:03.840
When the robot has finished loading it into the database,

35:03.840 --> 35:05.880
it emails me and Malloy.

35:05.880 --> 35:08.860
We see if there are any amended filings,

35:08.860 --> 35:11.920
if there are any new PACs, if anything's really changed,

35:11.920 --> 35:14.340
it'll summarize this in the email.

35:14.340 --> 35:18.800
Malloy can come in, see all of the new amended filings,

35:18.800 --> 35:22.360
check them manually against this horrible thing

35:22.360 --> 35:27.360
on the FEC's website by searching for the,

35:29.880 --> 35:33.520
the identifier, check a few boxes,

35:33.520 --> 35:35.480
make sure everything's kosher, hit publish,

35:35.480 --> 35:37.340
and then the graphic updates.

35:38.260 --> 35:40.400
So it's really important on something like this

35:40.400 --> 35:42.400
where you're relying on an external data source

35:42.400 --> 35:44.800
that you have a robot going to download and process

35:44.800 --> 35:48.560
that you actually do have a human who sort of sits there

35:48.560 --> 35:52.800
and looks at it and says, okay, everything looks good,

35:52.800 --> 35:56.120
everything's lining up, we're gonna hit publish.

35:56.320 --> 35:58.000
I would strongly recommend that.

35:59.600 --> 36:01.880
Other people have made a mistake.

36:01.880 --> 36:03.600
It's just a reminder that for all the stuff

36:03.600 --> 36:06.600
you do programmatically we still need common sense

36:06.600 --> 36:09.040
and knowledge and thought and so that,

36:09.040 --> 36:11.760
we kind of joke around about how they just push a button

36:11.760 --> 36:13.920
but the truth is there's almost none of that.

36:13.920 --> 36:16.760
Almost nothing that we do is that simple.

36:16.760 --> 36:18.520
It either required work on the front end

36:18.520 --> 36:22.600
like Ken's earthquake program to decide

36:22.600 --> 36:25.680
what was newsworthy and what should we exclude

36:25.880 --> 36:27.680
or on this stuff which is very complicated

36:27.680 --> 36:30.840
and we've spent a long time really learning

36:30.840 --> 36:33.600
the political campaign process

36:33.600 --> 36:35.280
and brings a lot of knowledge to it.

36:35.280 --> 36:38.960
So I mean Ben kind of jokes about like a robot reporter.

36:38.960 --> 36:43.520
The point is to take care of the busy work

36:43.520 --> 36:46.720
and reserve sort of the brain power and stuff

36:46.720 --> 36:49.320
that really matters to produce the quality content

36:49.320 --> 36:50.160
out the other end.

36:51.080 --> 36:56.080
Exactly and so this is a sort of database view

36:57.000 --> 36:59.240
of that CSV I was showing you earlier.

36:59.240 --> 37:01.960
This is all of the information we get from the FEC.

37:03.120 --> 37:05.760
We have a separate admin where we can for instance

37:05.760 --> 37:08.760
tie a pack to a specific candidate.

37:08.760 --> 37:11.320
So we do all of that in the back and then we hit go

37:11.320 --> 37:13.360
and then it spits out this nice page

37:14.600 --> 37:17.960
that is actually powered by an extremely robust database

37:17.960 --> 37:20.840
that we've spent a great deal of time developing

37:20.840 --> 37:22.920
and cleaning and checking.

37:22.920 --> 37:26.520
Malloy and I have double and triple and quadruple

37:26.520 --> 37:28.160
checked the accuracy of all of this

37:28.160 --> 37:29.840
on at least a number of occasions

37:31.120 --> 37:33.680
and we keep like going back and doing it again.

37:34.640 --> 37:35.840
So we know it's good.

37:38.160 --> 37:40.000
And this is sort of how you would arrive at that.

37:40.000 --> 37:41.420
It's a Django database.

37:41.420 --> 37:46.420
This is a very simple pack model

37:50.780 --> 37:52.220
where it's the Sierra Club.

37:52.220 --> 37:53.860
They have their FEC unique identifier

37:53.860 --> 37:55.500
and if we wanted to tie the Sierra Club

37:55.500 --> 37:57.340
to any one of these candidates we could.

38:01.060 --> 38:04.300
For those of you who don't have the programming

38:04.300 --> 38:09.300
and research resources to build your own database,

38:09.660 --> 38:12.780
write scripts to automate pulling in FEC data

38:12.780 --> 38:15.940
and have a researcher who has years and years of experience

38:15.940 --> 38:18.680
dealing with the FEC to go in and clean it up.

38:18.680 --> 38:22.740
The New York Times handily has a campaign finance API

38:22.740 --> 38:27.740
that actually ProPublica uses for this presentation

38:29.300 --> 38:31.460
that if you want to start playing around

38:31.460 --> 38:33.540
with some of this campaign finance data

38:33.540 --> 38:34.580
I would highly recommend it.

38:34.580 --> 38:39.140
I haven't spent a great deal of time in here myself

38:39.180 --> 38:41.700
although I've talked to a couple of the developers

38:41.700 --> 38:44.200
who made it and they are very smart people.

38:45.460 --> 38:50.460
And you can for instance come in and get a JSON list

38:52.280 --> 38:56.860
of the most recent expenditures that looks like this

38:56.860 --> 38:59.140
through the New York Times campaign finance API.

38:59.140 --> 39:00.720
You need an API key.

39:02.060 --> 39:04.460
I'm pretty sure anyone in this room could get one.

39:05.460 --> 39:10.460
And it's a very nice representation of the FEC data.

39:14.900 --> 39:18.200
I'm not positive the New York Times cleans it up

39:18.200 --> 39:20.780
in the same way that we do although I would imagine

39:20.780 --> 39:21.620
that they do.

39:23.180 --> 39:25.640
Probably something to look into if you're interested

39:25.640 --> 39:26.480
in using it.

39:29.180 --> 39:32.820
On the front end, so I talked about the database

39:32.820 --> 39:36.260
and stuff on the front end, this is all just JavaScript.

39:36.260 --> 39:38.620
So for those of you who know a little bit of HTML

39:38.620 --> 39:41.980
there's actually not a lot of fancy stuff going on

39:41.980 --> 39:44.060
like this is just a table.

39:44.060 --> 39:47.780
Like it's an HTML table which is handy

39:47.780 --> 39:50.820
because you're dealing with a graphic

39:50.820 --> 39:55.420
that it has huge sums of money that are changing

39:55.420 --> 39:57.380
and growing every single day.

39:57.380 --> 39:59.820
And you have this opposed column on one side

39:59.820 --> 40:01.420
and a support column on the other side

40:01.420 --> 40:03.940
and you need to make sure that everything is fitting

40:03.940 --> 40:08.120
into 980 pixels and that the bars are all sized appropriately

40:08.120 --> 40:09.920
for the space you have and the amount of money

40:09.920 --> 40:11.420
they're supposed to represent.

40:13.420 --> 40:17.140
Django, which is the software we use sort of on the backend

40:17.140 --> 40:22.220
to develop this, has some very handy features

40:22.220 --> 40:23.360
that you can apply on the template

40:23.360 --> 40:26.060
that will automatically resize your divs

40:26.060 --> 40:28.940
based on a value and a maximum width, right?

40:28.940 --> 40:32.820
So in this case the maximum width is 980 pixels

40:32.820 --> 40:36.020
and your value is 20 million dollars

40:36.020 --> 40:40.540
and then your maximum value is 33 million dollars,

40:40.540 --> 40:42.540
34 million dollars.

40:42.540 --> 40:46.260
So like the pixels on the div are actually

40:46.260 --> 40:48.220
completely representative of the amount of money

40:48.220 --> 40:49.540
that they're supposed to have

40:49.540 --> 40:51.660
and everything sizes automatically.

40:51.660 --> 40:53.760
And all of that is sort of handled for me.

40:55.020 --> 40:57.820
The table adjusts automatically if more

40:57.860 --> 40:59.700
falls under the oppose or the support

40:59.700 --> 41:02.420
because an HTML table is just built that way.

41:03.860 --> 41:05.700
The divs all size appropriately

41:05.700 --> 41:07.500
because we have some code on the backend

41:07.500 --> 41:11.580
and a full featured piece of software

41:11.580 --> 41:13.860
to sort of handle that for us.

41:15.420 --> 41:18.260
And then, how many of you have heard of jQuery?

41:19.660 --> 41:21.580
Right, so if you don't know JavaScript

41:21.580 --> 41:24.340
or don't have a lot of front end development experience,

41:25.340 --> 41:29.140
you can actually sort of get a long way using jQuery.

41:29.140 --> 41:34.140
Like this, this was a feature we added later

41:34.580 --> 41:37.480
but we have the date picker and slider.

41:40.020 --> 41:42.060
Somebody had written a jQuery plugin

41:42.060 --> 41:45.820
for a slider by date range, or not by date range,

41:45.820 --> 41:47.500
just for a slider, right?

41:47.500 --> 41:50.140
And I went in and I lightly modified it

41:51.020 --> 41:53.020
to accommodate what I needed

41:53.020 --> 41:56.300
and in less than a day's work,

41:56.300 --> 41:59.260
it was working for my specific application, right?

41:59.260 --> 42:03.140
So chances are if you have a feature in mind

42:03.140 --> 42:06.420
or you want to sort of accomplish something technically,

42:06.420 --> 42:08.660
someone has already kind of done a lot of the work

42:08.660 --> 42:10.120
for you and open sourced it.

42:12.020 --> 42:13.700
And that was true here.

42:13.700 --> 42:18.700
So my focus here was writing some JavaScript

42:19.380 --> 42:24.380
to handle the math to sort of adjust all of these numbers

42:24.840 --> 42:29.300
and adjust the different sizes of the bars and the divs

42:29.300 --> 42:31.980
to accommodate the slide,

42:31.980 --> 42:34.260
but I didn't actually have to code the slider from scratch.

42:34.260 --> 42:36.040
So I got to focus on the actual problem,

42:36.040 --> 42:38.900
which was the math and making sure everything was adjusted

42:38.900 --> 42:43.220
and like the numbers and these tool tips update

42:43.220 --> 42:44.940
as you drag it, like, you know,

42:45.940 --> 42:49.540
making sure all of that worked as I wanted it to

42:49.540 --> 42:53.380
and tested that and not so much worry about the rest.

42:56.780 --> 43:01.780
We have some code on the backend also that takes, right,

43:02.860 --> 43:07.060
all of these different FEC expenditures and transactions

43:07.060 --> 43:08.340
we have stored in the database.

43:08.340 --> 43:11.740
It sort of looks at all of them in aggregate,

43:11.740 --> 43:16.740
does some math and spits out a JSON feed onto this page

43:18.480 --> 43:20.700
that we then use to render the graphic.

43:20.700 --> 43:22.360
Does anybody know what JSON is?

43:24.500 --> 43:28.860
Okay, it's a way of sort of serializing your data

43:28.860 --> 43:30.900
so that it comes out in a structured,

43:30.900 --> 43:34.060
easy to use way on the front end.

43:34.060 --> 43:37.460
Much like this, right?

43:37.460 --> 43:42.460
So instead of some crazy, right, like FEC spreadsheet,

43:44.700 --> 43:48.820
you actually sort of see the breakdown of the data

43:48.820 --> 43:50.180
and it's easy for you to come in

43:50.180 --> 43:52.340
and programmatically access and process.

43:54.420 --> 43:56.520
So that probably just about does it

43:56.520 --> 43:58.380
in terms of this presentation.

43:58.380 --> 44:00.140
I'm happy to answer questions about it

44:00.140 --> 44:01.900
or to show off some other stuff

44:01.900 --> 44:04.000
we've been doing with elections lately.

44:04.000 --> 44:04.840
So, yeah.

44:06.280 --> 44:08.880
Just the qualifying, if an ad is

44:08.880 --> 44:11.240
definitively opposing one candidate

44:11.240 --> 44:12.380
or supporting another candidate,

44:12.380 --> 44:14.440
because some apps do die and kind of split,

44:14.440 --> 44:16.360
even though the 30 seconds kind of split the time,

44:16.360 --> 44:19.160
is that an editorial decision that you see the FEC make?

44:19.160 --> 44:21.220
Luckily, that is not a call we make, right?

44:21.220 --> 44:26.220
So the FEC in their filing requirements

44:26.920 --> 44:29.300
makes them pick, right?

44:29.300 --> 44:30.320
So they have this neat column

44:30.320 --> 44:32.560
in their spreadsheet support repose.

