47:04.340 --> 47:05.940
And there's all sorts of things to like,

47:05.940 --> 47:07.060
you know, like there's this whole debate.

47:07.060 --> 47:08.580
Can we give Bachman pink?

47:08.580 --> 47:09.540
We had to switch that.

47:09.540 --> 47:10.820
You know, you're gonna make her pink, you know,

47:10.820 --> 47:12.020
and then, you know, you went right.

47:12.020 --> 47:14.820
So it was a big debate, but that's what it is.

47:14.820 --> 47:17.780
It's basically trying to be consistent from here

47:17.780 --> 47:19.020
throughout the rest of the products

47:19.020 --> 47:20.620
that we created for the campaign.

47:21.660 --> 47:23.180
I can tell there's probably other people in the room

47:23.180 --> 47:24.420
who have faced this problem.

47:25.220 --> 47:26.060
It's very, very fun.

47:29.980 --> 47:31.140
Any other questions?

47:33.260 --> 47:37.100
I wonder if how many LA Times journalists source this data?

47:37.100 --> 47:39.300
Also, do you see other journalists

47:39.300 --> 47:42.460
that are doing this data?

47:42.460 --> 47:44.260
All right, so the question is how many LA Times

47:44.260 --> 47:45.900
journalists are sourcing this data

47:45.900 --> 47:49.260
and what other organizations are doing the same thing?

47:49.260 --> 47:54.260
Okay, how many of us work on it,

47:55.620 --> 47:57.060
on elections or super PACs?

48:06.020 --> 48:08.660
Yeah, we actually field requests from reporters

48:08.660 --> 48:11.940
and our DC Bureau on a fairly regular basis,

48:11.940 --> 48:13.840
particularly with campaign finance.

48:14.780 --> 48:16.700
Malloy does a lot of that.

48:16.700 --> 48:18.500
Doug Smith and Sandra Poindexter

48:18.660 --> 48:19.820
also do a lot of that.

48:21.540 --> 48:23.200
I sort of have the keys to the database

48:23.200 --> 48:25.620
on the super PAC expenditures.

48:25.620 --> 48:27.540
So I help out from time to time

48:27.540 --> 48:29.960
when there's a super PAC specific question.

48:33.220 --> 48:37.040
So the number of people who are looking at

48:37.040 --> 48:40.500
and cleaning up and analyzing this kind of data,

48:40.500 --> 48:44.660
five or six maybe, including Malloy, Doug Smith,

48:44.660 --> 48:47.180
Sandy Poindexter, and then Ken and me

48:47.180 --> 48:51.100
and then other organizations.

48:51.100 --> 48:52.660
New York Times is doing it.

48:53.700 --> 48:55.080
Open Secrets does it.

48:57.420 --> 48:58.300
Anyone else?

49:00.340 --> 49:01.940
I'm sure there's other people.

49:01.940 --> 49:05.300
I think every lot of organizations are playing too

49:05.300 --> 49:07.980
because we all know that the kind of money

49:07.980 --> 49:11.820
that's going to be spent during this campaign cycle,

49:11.820 --> 49:16.300
why people have it, is kind of hard to wrap it all around.

49:16.300 --> 49:20.020
They're already very close to 100 million dollars.

49:20.020 --> 49:20.860
And that's not even talking about

49:20.860 --> 49:23.500
what the management committee themselves is going to do.

49:23.500 --> 49:26.020
It's going to be a lot of money spent.

49:26.020 --> 49:29.860
So our goal here was to try and get people an idea

49:29.860 --> 49:32.260
of how much money is being spent

49:32.260 --> 49:34.620
and how it is being spent.

49:34.620 --> 49:37.340
And you can see in this example here

49:37.340 --> 49:41.100
that we're on the test with the past affiliated with law

49:41.100 --> 49:44.700
and we are in support of the expense of a lot of money

49:44.820 --> 49:49.100
and we have to get rid of the

49:49.100 --> 49:49.980
I mean a lot of money.

49:49.980 --> 49:52.040
It was fascinating to watch with the timeline.

49:52.040 --> 49:54.100
You could see a moment in time when all of a sudden

49:54.100 --> 49:56.780
one of me turned his attention to the timeline.

49:56.780 --> 49:58.900
He no longer cared about the image.

49:59.820 --> 50:01.900
We could watch it and we got our interest in Europe.

50:01.900 --> 50:04.420
We were like, you just had a white coat.

50:04.420 --> 50:06.380
Now, you could see this moment in time.

50:06.380 --> 50:09.900
It was very clear that there had been a shift

50:09.900 --> 50:12.620
and that English was no longer a threat.

50:13.220 --> 50:16.260
For that, this has been really useful.

50:16.260 --> 50:20.460
I think it's really a good tool to try and explain

50:20.460 --> 50:24.460
how this campaign is going to play out with super PAC money.

50:24.460 --> 50:27.580
That was kind of our motivation in using it.

50:27.580 --> 50:30.100
I think a lot of other organizations

50:30.100 --> 50:33.940
are trying to use super PACs to tell the story

50:33.940 --> 50:35.380
and do it in different ways.

50:35.380 --> 50:40.380
Do you talk about, if you remember,

50:42.940 --> 50:44.380
what can you first, if you will,

50:44.380 --> 50:46.420
in terms of the branding, design,

50:46.420 --> 50:50.580
when you knew that the story was about spending a post-up,

50:50.580 --> 50:52.700
or did you go through data first and then say,

50:52.700 --> 50:55.020
this is the story we are going to tell,

50:55.020 --> 50:55.860
then you go to find it.

50:55.860 --> 50:56.940
Does that make sense?

50:56.940 --> 51:00.060
It does and it was a lot of both.

51:00.060 --> 51:02.260
We had looked at what some of our competitors were doing.

51:02.260 --> 51:04.940
We looked at ProPublica, we looked at MIT, OpenSecrets,

51:04.940 --> 51:06.140
a couple other people,

51:09.260 --> 51:12.380
and saw what they had done.

51:12.380 --> 51:17.380
It's, in some cases, a little bit difficult to come here

51:17.500 --> 51:20.060
and quickly process what's happening.

51:23.180 --> 51:25.740
We knew we wanted to tell a story.

51:25.740 --> 51:29.420
We didn't want to have a data dump

51:29.420 --> 51:31.860
that a reader or a viewer would have to come in

51:31.860 --> 51:34.260
and sift through in order to figure out

51:35.340 --> 51:37.380
what's going on in the world.

51:37.380 --> 51:42.380
So, we had this philosophy like,

51:42.380 --> 51:43.820
we don't have as many resources

51:43.820 --> 51:46.820
as some of the other news organizations out there.

51:46.820 --> 51:49.620
We're a small team, three developers,

51:50.460 --> 51:54.220
three people who analyze and play with data.

51:56.780 --> 52:00.340
We can't do everything and be everything all the time.

52:00.340 --> 52:02.100
So, a lot of the times, we want to try and focus

52:02.100 --> 52:04.540
on one really great thing,

52:05.540 --> 52:08.620
one really great part of the data that we can show off,

52:08.620 --> 52:10.580
one really great interactive or feature

52:11.580 --> 52:13.940
that we can do and do really well

52:13.940 --> 52:15.140
rather than trying to,

52:17.660 --> 52:22.660
rather than doing the whole thing.

52:22.660 --> 52:26.260
Like, you know, in this case, or in this case,

52:26.260 --> 52:29.220
like ProPublica has this really great tree map

52:29.220 --> 52:33.140
of the biggest donors that we haven't,

52:33.140 --> 52:34.260
we would very much like to do

52:34.260 --> 52:35.900
but have not gotten around to yet.

52:37.780 --> 52:39.740
This is another example of something like that

52:39.740 --> 52:43.180
where we just launched this recently.

52:44.380 --> 52:47.580
You know, the idea here was we wanted to give people

52:47.580 --> 52:51.460
information about what states were considered

52:51.460 --> 52:54.180
at play in the election.

52:55.100 --> 52:59.540
So, we have our wonderful DC Bureau

52:59.540 --> 53:03.660
wrote a very informative blurb about each state.

53:03.660 --> 53:05.820
And we've made editorial selections

53:05.820 --> 53:09.140
about which states are considered battleground states

53:09.140 --> 53:14.140
that the reader can come in and turn either Obama or Romney

53:14.260 --> 53:18.100
to see what the results could be.

53:19.700 --> 53:22.580
You know, and the Huffington Post and the New York Times

53:22.780 --> 53:24.780
and other news organizations have done a lot more

53:24.780 --> 53:26.100
with this kind of data.

53:26.100 --> 53:28.500
We wanted to make something that was both informative

53:28.500 --> 53:32.140
and fun, something that focused you on

53:33.620 --> 53:36.940
what was really the story, what was really going on,

53:36.940 --> 53:39.740
rather than giving you a ton of different options

53:39.740 --> 53:42.980
and sort of overwhelming the user.

53:42.980 --> 53:47.300
But, you know, so our philosophy on Super PACs was,

53:47.300 --> 53:51.860
let's tell a story, let's come in and find a unique way

53:51.940 --> 53:54.340
of presenting the data and telling the best story

53:54.340 --> 53:56.820
about what was interesting.

53:56.820 --> 53:59.340
So, we looked at what our competitors had done.

53:59.340 --> 54:00.620
We looked at the raw data.

54:00.620 --> 54:03.140
We sat around the table for a long time,

54:03.140 --> 54:05.660
sort of batting back and forth different ways

54:05.660 --> 54:09.200
of presenting it and different things to break it down by.

54:10.100 --> 54:12.660
And a lot of it was dictated by what the FEC gave us, right?

54:12.660 --> 54:15.260
So, they gave us support and oppose.

54:15.260 --> 54:18.180
We knew pretty well which PACs were affiliated

54:18.180 --> 54:19.300
with each candidate.

54:20.180 --> 54:23.280
And we decided we wanted to look at how

54:23.280 --> 54:25.340
those PACs were spending on other candidates.

54:25.340 --> 54:28.380
And we, you know, you kind of had an idea at the outset

54:28.380 --> 54:30.580
that it would probably be a lot of negative.

54:32.000 --> 54:33.240
And it was.

54:33.240 --> 54:34.620
And, you know, you come to this page

54:34.620 --> 54:36.400
and you immediately see that Rami has just spent

54:36.400 --> 54:40.620
exorbitant amounts of money opposing Santorum and Gingrich.

54:42.220 --> 54:46.020
And this was sort of,

54:47.020 --> 54:50.060
this kind of storytelling is what we were after.

54:50.060 --> 54:52.900
So, it was, you know, a combination of

54:52.900 --> 54:54.880
what's the best way of presenting the data?

54:54.880 --> 54:57.740
What do we have already that's available?

54:57.740 --> 55:00.580
And what looking at it do we think is interesting

55:00.580 --> 55:02.340
or is going to be interesting?

55:02.340 --> 55:05.260
Both, if the raw data already and just sort of knowing,

55:05.260 --> 55:07.580
having that knowledge of what we thought

55:07.580 --> 55:08.820
was probably gonna happen.

55:09.820 --> 55:12.420
I was just wondering if there was an exception

55:12.420 --> 55:14.620
in the equation, how many people

55:14.620 --> 55:18.860
were there to be required for the project?

55:18.860 --> 55:19.700
That's a good question.

55:19.700 --> 55:23.700
So, the question is from, you know,

55:23.700 --> 55:25.860
the first idea to launch, basically,

55:25.860 --> 55:27.860
how much time and resources and people

55:27.860 --> 55:29.580
were required for the project.

55:31.620 --> 55:36.540
I did pretty much all of the coding front and back.

55:36.540 --> 55:39.980
Malloy did a tremendous amount of work,

55:39.980 --> 55:42.700
initially cleaning up all of the FEC data

55:42.700 --> 55:44.460
and continually, every single day,

55:44.460 --> 55:46.580
every time we update it, it's coming in

55:46.580 --> 55:50.220
and doing work to clean up the filings.

55:51.620 --> 55:52.460
A few weeks.

55:54.620 --> 55:57.020
So, it was a pretty quick turnaround, considering.

55:58.060 --> 56:00.220
You talked about the secular sub-product,

56:00.220 --> 56:04.180
whether some of these people are using, you know,

56:04.420 --> 56:06.420
some of these people.

56:06.420 --> 56:07.260
Sure.

56:08.340 --> 56:11.420
Yeah, I decided to learn to code because I wanted a job.

56:11.420 --> 56:14.300
I, you know, I came here as an intern

56:14.300 --> 56:17.380
and I was a web intern and I got lucky enough

56:17.380 --> 56:21.060
to be placed with Megan on the data team, writing.

56:21.060 --> 56:22.620
Is it on camera?

56:22.620 --> 56:23.460
This is on camera.

56:23.460 --> 56:25.660
I was lucky enough to be placed with Megan Garvey

56:25.660 --> 56:26.500
as an intern.

56:26.500 --> 56:31.500
And sort of looked at Ben and looked at Ken

56:35.460 --> 56:37.780
when he came in as an intern also

56:37.780 --> 56:39.820
and sort of figured like, you know,

56:39.820 --> 56:41.100
the company's bankrupt.

56:41.100 --> 56:43.420
They're not gonna hire me unless I have

56:43.420 --> 56:45.540
seriously marketable skills, right?

56:45.540 --> 56:48.620
So, I worked on the skills.

56:48.620 --> 56:51.620
I started off, I sort of already knew a little bit of HTML,

56:51.620 --> 56:54.620
no JavaScript, so I polished my HTML skills,

56:54.620 --> 56:55.940
picked up jQuery.

56:55.940 --> 56:59.180
From there, I learned more JavaScript.

57:01.580 --> 57:02.420
Through books.

57:02.420 --> 57:05.420
Books and websites like W3schools is really good

57:05.420 --> 57:07.520
for HTML in particular.

57:09.580 --> 57:12.640
And then from there, I learned Python and then Django.

57:16.180 --> 57:17.080
Two years?

57:21.500 --> 57:22.340
Three years?

57:23.260 --> 57:25.260
Something in there.

57:29.500 --> 57:30.320
Oh, nine.

57:31.300 --> 57:32.660
Yeah.

57:32.660 --> 57:33.900
Since 2009.

57:35.580 --> 57:36.420
Takes a while.

57:39.940 --> 57:40.780
Yes.

57:53.260 --> 57:54.460
Absolutely.

57:54.460 --> 57:56.580
I think it's pretty rare that we launch

57:56.580 --> 57:59.720
a fully baked product right off the bat.

58:02.700 --> 58:04.180
And we're trying to sort of get away

58:04.180 --> 58:06.180
from that attitude as well.

58:06.180 --> 58:09.580
But no, this, it launched sort of as a more

58:09.580 --> 58:11.020
static version of this.

58:11.020 --> 58:15.440
The date range picker and slider was not here.

58:16.380 --> 58:19.920
But, like, this stuff was.

58:23.140 --> 58:25.140
Oh, right, this, right.

58:29.380 --> 58:34.380
The sort of rollover where you could see how a candidate

58:35.460 --> 58:37.740
get highlighted on it was added later.

58:37.740 --> 58:41.400
The date range slider was added later.

58:41.400 --> 58:44.700
But it launched looking a lot like this.

58:46.420 --> 58:51.020
And we'd like to add, like, it's nice to see the events

58:51.060 --> 58:52.740
that precipitated some of the spending.

58:52.740 --> 58:54.540
So like the South Carolina primary,

58:54.540 --> 58:56.860
or the Gingrich winning South Carolina,

58:56.860 --> 58:58.940
or St. John dropping out of the race.

58:58.940 --> 59:00.340
We've got some space issues.

59:00.340 --> 59:03.580
Anthony actually cleaned that up earlier today.

59:03.580 --> 59:06.140
So I think it's always a work in progress.

59:06.140 --> 59:06.980
I've never done that.

59:06.980 --> 59:09.700
And yeah, the other thing is we have this great database now

59:09.700 --> 59:11.820
of all of these expenditures and super packs.

59:11.820 --> 59:14.280
And we have one page, right, which is great.

59:14.280 --> 59:18.580
But it'd be nice if we had detail pages on each pack.

59:18.580 --> 59:21.380
It'd be nice if we could do a little bit more

59:21.380 --> 59:25.660
and tell a few more stories with the enormous amount

59:25.660 --> 59:29.560
of data we have and the great database we have from the FEC.

59:29.560 --> 59:32.320
So certainly there's a plan to do more.

59:32.320 --> 59:34.900
What it's gonna be yet is still a little bit up in the air.

59:34.900 --> 59:36.980
But this was an evolution.

59:36.980 --> 59:40.460
And then there will be more with the data later

59:40.460 --> 59:42.360
after the California primary, probably.

59:46.220 --> 59:47.060
Yes.

59:48.820 --> 59:51.820
Is there a lot of response to this?

59:51.820 --> 59:55.040
Or is there anything that you'd like to say?

59:55.040 --> 59:57.640
We've gotten some great response on this.

59:59.920 --> 01:00:01.840
Apparently not a lot of comments, but.

01:00:01.840 --> 01:00:06.840
But this map though, there are so many comments.

01:00:09.600 --> 01:00:10.440
It's really.

01:00:13.480 --> 01:00:16.020
But stuff like this, it does well.

01:00:16.460 --> 01:00:20.340
And I sit next to our homepage producers

01:00:20.340 --> 01:00:23.760
and can badger them to link to more interactive

01:00:23.760 --> 01:00:26.580
and database-driven work that we're doing.

01:00:26.580 --> 01:00:31.280
And we regularly are sending emails to all of our bloggers

01:00:31.280 --> 01:00:34.420
and reporters in DC to get stuff linked up

01:00:34.420 --> 01:00:36.260
that we do on their posts.

01:00:37.460 --> 01:00:40.900
So it's an ongoing effort to get even our stuff promoted

01:00:40.900 --> 01:00:43.880
across the website in the way that it should be.

01:00:43.880 --> 01:00:47.200
But when people do find it, it does really well.

01:00:47.200 --> 01:00:50.920
And people spend a lot of time on these pages.

01:00:50.920 --> 01:00:55.060
And they do well on their own, just in terms of page views.

01:00:56.060 --> 01:00:58.560
We have a much longer half-life story.

01:00:58.560 --> 01:01:02.560
The story's gonna get 99% views per two hours.

01:01:04.240 --> 01:01:06.440
Something like this can be linked to stories

01:01:06.440 --> 01:01:08.920
that come from the website.

01:01:08.920 --> 01:01:12.000
And then they actually have a lot more answers,

01:01:12.120 --> 01:01:16.620
but we're not always allowed to be linked to versions

01:01:16.620 --> 01:01:19.700
of the stuff like this under them.

01:01:19.700 --> 01:01:23.860
After all, it's gonna be linked to stories like this.

01:01:27.240 --> 01:01:28.080
Yeah.

01:01:28.080 --> 01:01:29.320
Sorry, so.

01:01:29.320 --> 01:01:31.600
The question is about how much of the work that we do

01:01:31.600 --> 01:01:32.500
makes it back into print.

01:01:32.500 --> 01:01:34.880
And Ben showed a couple examples early on of that

01:01:34.880 --> 01:01:39.080
where does this precise graphic make it into print?

01:01:39.080 --> 01:01:40.160
This has not yet.

01:01:40.160 --> 01:01:42.280
There might be other examples where it has.

01:01:42.280 --> 01:01:46.320
But I think that we've really worked to try to do more,

01:01:46.320 --> 01:01:49.360
to inform more of print by some of the web work

01:01:49.360 --> 01:01:53.440
that we're doing and not be so dictated

01:01:53.440 --> 01:01:55.440
by what we would do for print anyway.

01:01:55.440 --> 01:01:58.960
So that's definitely a struggle that has gone on.

01:01:58.960 --> 01:02:01.720
And the thing is, so like what Ben was saying,

01:02:01.720 --> 01:02:04.040
where there's a longer half-life, where this stuff lives on,

01:02:04.040 --> 01:02:05.360
where this stuff, you know what I mean,

01:02:05.360 --> 01:02:09.800
we're gonna eventually boil this down to Romney and Obama.

01:02:09.800 --> 01:02:11.200
And it'll change in that sense

01:02:11.200 --> 01:02:13.800
because the whole focus of what's going on

01:02:13.800 --> 01:02:16.980
in the news right now is about to change significantly

01:02:16.980 --> 01:02:18.960
as we look forward to November.

01:02:20.200 --> 01:02:21.840
But yeah, I mean that's definitely something

01:02:21.840 --> 01:02:24.800
that I think as an organization we're trying

01:02:24.800 --> 01:02:27.440
to think more creatively and think differently

01:02:27.440 --> 01:02:29.000
about how we produce the news

01:02:29.000 --> 01:02:30.900
so that the online stuff doesn't just stay online

01:02:30.900 --> 01:02:32.280
and the print stuff doesn't just stay in print,

01:02:32.280 --> 01:02:34.480
that there's much more communication back and forth.

01:02:34.480 --> 01:02:36.320
And I think Martin is ready to come up

01:02:36.320 --> 01:02:38.200
and bid you farewell.

01:02:40.720 --> 01:02:42.000
Thank you, Anthony.

01:02:42.000 --> 01:02:42.840
Very much.

01:02:42.840 --> 01:02:46.200
And we're gonna wrap up.

01:02:46.200 --> 01:02:47.440
We're gonna put the screen up.

01:02:47.440 --> 01:02:48.760
We're gonna open the bar again.

01:02:48.760 --> 01:02:50.840
And I just wanna thank you again for coming

01:02:50.840 --> 01:02:52.920
and join the ONA.

01:02:52.920 --> 01:02:55.280
I know I will, eventually, someday.

01:02:56.440 --> 01:03:00.480
Thank you, Julie, for the idea of this

01:03:00.480 --> 01:03:03.600
and for convincing us to do it.

01:03:03.600 --> 01:03:06.000
We had a good time and hope you all did too.

01:03:06.000 --> 01:03:09.880
And we'll be around, pinhole us, talk to us,

01:03:10.800 --> 01:03:15.640
follow us on Twitter, and so on.

01:03:15.640 --> 01:03:17.800
But thank you very much for coming.

01:03:17.800 --> 01:03:19.080
Thank you.
